### Step 3 — Notebook 03: Data Preprocessing

#### [MODIFY] [03_data_preprocessing.ipynb](file:///d:/MLPs/Home Credit Defaut Risk Predictor/03_data_preprocessing.ipynb)

Missing value handling additions:
| Column | Strategy | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `EXT_SOURCE_1` | Median (from train) | 56% missing — median is robust |
| `EXT_SOURCE_2` | Median (from train) | <0.2% missing |
| `EXT_SOURCE_3` | Median (from train) | ~20% missing — median is robust |

Feature transformations:
| Transform | Action |
|---|---|
| `DAYS_BIRTH → AGE` | `AGE = abs(DAYS_BIRTH) / 365.25`, then drop `DAYS_BIRTH` |
| `DAYS_EMPLOYED → YEARS_EMPLOYED` | Replace anomalous `365243` with `0`, then `YEARS_EMPLOYED = abs(DAYS_EMPLOYED) / 365.25`, then drop `DAYS_EMPLOYED` |

Feature encoding additions:
| Encoding | Column | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| **One-Hot** | `NAME_HOUSING_TYPE` | 6 categories, no inherent order |
| **Frequency/Target Encoding** | `ORGANIZATION_TYPE` | 58 categories — too many for one-hot; use target encoding (mean of TARGET per category, computed on train only) |

> [!NOTE]
> For `ORGANIZATION_TYPE` with 58 unique values, one-hot encoding would add 57 columns. We'll use **target encoding** instead (replacing each category with the mean default rate from the training set). This is more compact and often more predictive for high-cardinality categoricals.

Output: updated `train_preprocessed.csv` and `test_preprocessed.csv`

